package com.openinglab.shared.data

data class OpeningGuide(val white: String, val black: String, val structure: String, val pitfalls: String, val specific: Boolean = true) {
    fun plan(whiteSide: Boolean) = (if (whiteSide) white else black) + "\nStructure and breaks: " + structure + "\nWatch for: " + pitfalls
}

/** Original Ashva family guidance. Conditional plans are not engine-certified advice at every node. */
object OpeningGuides {
    private fun g(w: String, b: String, s: String, p: String) = OpeningGuide(w, b, s, p)
    private val guides = linkedMapOf(
        "Ruy Lopez" to g("Preserve or deliberately exchange the Spanish bishop, support e4 and prepare a well-supported d4 break. In closed structures compare a knight route through d2–f1 with queenside development.", "Contest e4 while developing. Choose a closed centre or an early central exchange deliberately; make the d5 break only when the resulting exchanges work.", "Closed Spanish positions often revolve around c3–d4 for White and ...d5 for Black. Exchange and open variations create different structures, so do not reuse a closed-line plan blindly.", "Bb5 does not usually win e5 immediately. The Berlin endgame, Marshall pawn sacrifice and Exchange structure need different handling."),
        "Sicilian Defense" to g("Use development and the central space from e4. In open lines, compare pressure on the d-file, a kingside attack and the timing of f4 or e5; check Black's queenside counterplay.", "Use the c-pawn exchange to create asymmetry and half-open c-file play. Choose ...d5 or ...e5 counterplay according to development and your pawn structure.", "Open Sicilians often give White a kingside majority and Black a central majority. Closed and Alapin routes do not have the same plans as open lines.", "Opposite-side attacks are races, not automatic wins. Do not transplant a Dragon attack into a Najdorf or assume every ...d5 break is legal or safe."),
        "French Defense" to g("Use the space of an e5 chain when present, but support d4 and develop before attacking. In exchange structures seek activity rather than relying on a space advantage that is no longer there.", "Attack the base of a white d4–e5 chain with ...c5 and consider ...f6 against its head. Find a role for the c8 bishop before the position opens.", "Advance structures fix d4/e5 against d5/e6; exchanges can remove that chain. Pawn breaks change the bishops' scope.", "An unsupported d4 pawn and a blocked c8 bishop are recurring issues. Calculate pressure on b2 and the king before pushing wing pawns."),
        "Caro-Kann Defense" to g("Develop actively and choose whether to retain an e5 space advantage or exchange on d5. In Panov structures coordinate pieces around the isolated d-pawn.", "Use ...c6 to support ...d5. Develop the c8 bishop outside the chain when the position permits, then challenge the centre without neglecting development.", "Advance, exchange and Panov lines produce distinct pawn structures. Typical counterplay involves ...c5 or ...e5 when sufficiently supported.", "The early bishop can become a target. In isolated-pawn positions activity and blockade matter more than calling the pawn simply weak."),
        "Italian Game" to g("Develop the bishop to c4, castle and decide between a slow c3–d3 setup and an immediate d4 break. Coordinate pressure near f7 instead of launching an unsupported attack.", "Develop and protect the e5 centre. Use ...d5 when prepared or meet a slow setup with matching development and central tension.", "Closed Italian centres allow manoeuvring; early d4 exchanges open lines quickly. Timing matters more than memorizing one fixed setup.", "An attack on f7 may be defended tactically. Early Ng5 and gambits require exact calculation, not just the quiet Italian plan."),
        "London System" to g("Develop Bf4 before closing its diagonal with e3, support d4 and compare e4 with Ne5 plans. Adapt c3, c4 and the bishop retreat to Black's actual setup.", "Challenge d4 with ...c5 and consider pressure on b2 or a bishop exchange. Complete development; do not assume White's setup removes the need for central counterplay.", "A d4–e3–c3 triangle is one setup, not a mandatory move order. ...c5 and ...Qb6 can demand concrete changes.", "Automatically playing the same moves can leave b2 or d4 loose. A kingside pawn attack needs a stable centre and safe king."),
        "Queen's Gambit Declined" to g("Maintain useful central tension and develop the c1 bishop. In exchange structures compare a minority attack with a central e4 expansion.", "Support d5, develop efficiently and work toward ...c5 or ...e5. Solve the c8 bishop's development without conceding the centre carelessly.", "The Carlsbad structure after cxd5/exd5 differs from hanging-pawn and isolated-pawn structures after other exchanges.", "Trading on d5 changes the long-term plan. A minority attack is conditional, not a forced sequence in every QGD line."),
        "Queen's Gambit Accepted" to g("Recover or compensate for the c4 pawn with development and central occupation. Compare e4 with e3/Bxc4 setups according to Black's timing.", "Do not cling to c4 at any cost. Use the time White spends recovering it to develop and challenge the centre.", "Early ...dxc4 removes Black's d-pawn from the centre; ...c5 or ...e5 can provide counterplay.", "Trying to keep an extra pawn with repeated queenside pawn moves may expose pieces and diagonals."),
        "Queen's Gambit" to g("Use c4 to challenge d5 and build development around the resulting central tension.", "Choose whether to accept, support or exchange the c4 challenge, then develop consistently with that choice.", "Acceptance, ...e6 support and ...c6 support lead to different structures; study the named branches separately.", "The c4 offer is not permission for either side to ignore development."),
        "Slav Defense" to g("Develop while deciding whether cxd5 helps your plan. Be ready to recover c4 if Black captures and supports it.", "Support d5 with ...c6 while keeping the c8 bishop's diagonal initially open. Balance developing that bishop with the safety of b7 and d5.", "The ...c6–d5 chain supports the centre; ...dxc4 changes the structure and can create a queenside pawn race.", "Developing the bishop too early can make b7 vulnerable. Pawn recovery needs calculation rather than a fixed move order."),
        "Semi-Slav Defense" to g("Choose between quiet development and a sharp central challenge. Prepare e4 and calculate the c4 capture before committing.", "The ...c6–d5–e6 triangle supports d5 but restricts the c8 bishop. Prepare ...c5 or ...e5 and activate that bishop when the centre allows.", "Meran and anti-Meran lines can open the centre rapidly; quiet exchange plans are not interchangeable with them.", "Sharp pawn grabs and centre breaks often contain forcing tactics; do not infer safety from the triangle alone."),
        "King's Indian Defense" to g("Use the broad centre, develop and decide whether to close it with d5 or retain tension. In closed centres compare queenside expansion with restraining Black's kingside play.", "Develop with ...g6/...Bg7 and challenge the centre with ...e5 or ...c5. In a closed centre, kingside counterplay can be possible, but first assess White's queenside threats.", "A closed d5/e4 centre differs from exchange and fianchetto structures. Opposite-wing plans depend on where the centre is locked.", "A kingside pawn storm is not appropriate in every branch, especially if central lines can open against your king."),
        "Grünfeld Defense" to g("Use central space and support the pawn centre with active pieces. Calculate whether advancing or exchanging a central pawn improves coordination.", "Attack the white centre with pieces and ...c5 rather than merely matching its pawn occupation. The g7 bishop's diagonal matters when central exchanges open it.", "Exchange lines often leave White a d4/e4 centre and Black active pressure against it.", "More central pawns do not guarantee an advantage; loose pawns and an exposed king can outweigh space."),
        "Nimzo-Indian Defense" to g("Resolve the c3 pin deliberately and decide whether the bishop pair or pawn structure is more valuable in the chosen line. Prepare central expansion with coordinated pieces.", "Use ...Bb4 pressure to influence White's e4 advance. Decide when exchanging on c3 is worth conceding the bishop pair.", "Doubled c-pawns, isolated d-pawns and clean centres can all arise; the structure after the exchange determines the plan.", "The pin is not permanent. Do not label doubled pawns automatically bad or the bishop pair automatically winning."),
        "Queen's Indian" to g("Develop and contest central squares while watching the b7 bishop's diagonal. Prepare e4 only with adequate support.", "Use ...b6/...Bb7 pressure on central dark squares and develop toward ...d5 or ...c5 counterplay.", "Flexible centres can transpose into other queen-pawn structures; track which central pawns remain.", "A fianchetto bishop may be blocked by your own centre; make pawn exchanges purposeful."),
        "Bogo-Indian" to g("Answer the bishop check while considering whether the block helps development or changes the bishop pair.", "Use ...Bb4+ to influence White's setup, then decide whether to exchange or retreat the bishop and challenge the centre.", "The checking bishop's fate affects dark-square control and White's central expansion.", "A check is not a gain by itself; repeated bishop moves may cost development time."),
        "Dutch Defense" to g("Develop against the dark-square weaknesses created by ...f5 and use central or queenside play according to Black's structure.", "Use ...f5 to influence e4 and decide between a Stonewall, classical or fianchetto structure. Finish development and preserve king safety.", "Stonewall pawns constrain both sides and often restrict the c8 bishop; other Dutch structures are more flexible.", "The f-pawn advance changes king shelter. Early tactical checks and e4 breaks can punish slow development."),
        "Benoni Defense" to g("Use space and central control while preparing a supported e5 or queenside plan. Restrain Black's queenside expansion when possible.", "Seek activity and queenside counterplay against White's space. Coordinate ...b5 and central pressure with your pieces.", "Modern Benoni structures often have White's d5 pawn against Black's d6 pawn, but other Benoni branches differ.", "A cramped position needs active counterplay; a premature pawn break can leave d6 or the king exposed."),
        "Benko Gambit" to g("Decide how much material to keep while neutralizing pressure on the a- and b-files. Complete development before trying to cash in the extra pawn.", "Use the pawn sacrifice to activate queenside files and the long diagonal. Maintain pressure without assuming the sacrifice guarantees compensation.", "Queenside pawn exchanges can create enduring file pressure even after queens are traded.", "The extra pawn is only one factor; file activity and safe development determine whether it can be converted."),
        "English Opening" to g("Use c4 to influence d5 and choose a flexible setup. Compare a d4 transposition with queenside expansion or a reversed Sicilian structure.", "Choose central occupation or symmetrical play, then contest key squares with well-placed pieces.", "Symmetrical, reversed Sicilian and queen-pawn transpositions have different breaks; track the actual structure rather than only the opening name.", "A reversed opening with an extra tempo does not make every plan automatically stronger."),
        "Réti Opening" to g("Develop flexibly with Nf3 and challenge the centre with c4 or d4 at a useful moment. A fianchetto needs an active diagonal.", "Occupy or restrain the centre and be ready for White's later pawn challenge. Avoid overextending central pawns without support.", "Move-order transpositions are common; identify the current pawn structure before choosing a plan.", "Delaying central pawns is flexibility, not a reason to ignore central control."),
        "Catalan Opening" to g("Use the g2 bishop's long diagonal and queen-pawn space. Compare recovering c4 immediately with activity-based compensation.", "Develop against long-diagonal pressure and decide whether holding c4 is compatible with central stability and development.", "Open Catalan pawn captures differ from closed ...c6/...e6 structures; the g2 bishop needs a useful route through the centre.", "Long-diagonal pressure can be blocked by either side's pawns. An extra c4 pawn is not always safe to retain."),
        "Scotch Game" to g("Use the early d4 break to open the centre and develop actively. Choose knight placement with tempo and central control in mind.", "Develop efficiently against White's active centre. Calculate central exchanges and checks before chasing the advanced knight.", "The early d-pawn exchange creates open lines sooner than in closed king-pawn systems.", "Moving one piece repeatedly can surrender the development gained by opening the centre."),
        "Petrov's Defense" to g("Answer the counterattack on e4 and develop into the resulting symmetrical or unbalanced centre.", "Use ...Nf6 counterpressure, but calculate the order of pawn captures before assuming symmetry is safe.", "Central exchanges can lead to open e-files and symmetrical structures; activity still matters.", "The early e-pawn captures have tactical move-order details. Symmetry is not a drawing guarantee."),
        "Scandinavian Defense" to g("Use the time spent attacking Black's queen for development, then build central control without chasing it aimlessly.", "Challenge e4 immediately and choose queen placement or knight recapture consistent with safe development.", "The early d/e pawn exchange leaves central files open and makes piece activity important.", "A queen tempo matters only when your developing move is useful; repeated queen chasing can neglect the centre."),
        "Pirc Defense" to g("Build a supported centre and choose between quiet development and a sharper f4/f3 setup. Prepare attacks while monitoring central counterplay.", "Develop flexibly with ...d6 and a kingside fianchetto, then challenge the centre with ...c5 or ...e5.", "A large white pawn centre is a target as well as an asset; pawn-break timing determines its stability.", "Do not start a wing attack while a central break can open lines against your own king."),
        "Modern Defense" to g("Occupy and support the centre, then choose development that meets Black's delayed pawn challenge.", "Use flexible development and ...Bg7 pressure; choose central counterplay before White consolidates without opposition.", "Delayed knight and central-pawn commitments allow many transpositions, each with its own pawn structure.", "Flexibility can become passivity if the centre is never challenged."),
        "Alekhine Defense" to g("Use central space but avoid pushing pawns only to chase the knight. Develop and keep the centre supportable.", "Induce advances and attack the resulting centre with coordinated pieces and pawn breaks.", "White's advanced central pawns can gain space or become targets; exchanges determine which.", "Multiple pawn tempi do not automatically win space safely, and repeated knight retreats need a concrete purpose."),
        "King's Gambit" to g("Use the f-pawn offer to open lines and accelerate development. Check king safety and concrete recovery before assuming an attack.", "Decide whether to keep the pawn or return it for development. Use central counterplay against White's loosened kingside.", "The f-file and weakened e1–h4 diagonal can be important, especially before White castles.", "A gambit is not a forced win. Material retention and early king checks need exact calculation."),
        "Vienna" to g("Use Nc3 to support e4 and choose a quiet development plan or a calculated f4 challenge.", "Develop and challenge the centre; be ready for an early f-pawn advance rather than only mirroring moves.", "The f4 break can open attacking files but also changes king shelter.", "Do not assume every Vienna branch supports the same kingside attack."),
        "Four Knights" to g("Use sound development to prepare d4 or a useful bishop plan, rather than seeking an attack without a central basis.", "Complete development and challenge central space; use symmetry only while it remains tactically safe.", "Early central exchanges open files; quiet lines can transpose into Spanish or Scotch structures.", "Blindly copying moves can fail when checks or captures break the symmetry."),
        "Philidor" to g("Use space and development to pressure the centre. Prepare d4 and open lines when your pieces are ready.", "Support e5 with ...d6, develop out of the compact structure and prepare central counterplay.", "The ...d6/e5 setup can be solid but restricts pieces until a pawn break or exchange frees them.", "Passive piece placement can make the compact structure a lasting burden."),
        "Tarrasch Defense" to g("Develop against Black's active central play; if an isolated d-pawn appears, compare blockade and exchanges with your own king safety.", "Use ...c5 to contest the centre and active pieces to compensate if a central pawn becomes isolated.", "Isolated d-pawn structures reward activity before exchanges and blockade after activity has been reduced.", "Trading pieces indiscriminately can change whether the isolated pawn is an attacking asset or an endgame target."),
        "Trompowsky" to g("Use Bg5 to influence Black's knight and decide whether exchanging is worth altering Black's structure.", "Meet the bishop pressure with useful central play; decide whether to accept structural damage or avoid the exchange.", "Early bishop exchanges can leave doubled pawns and an open file, but outcomes depend on the chosen branch.", "The bishop move is not always a pin to the king; inspect the board before treating the knight as immobile."),
        "Torre Attack" to g("Develop with Nf3/Bg5 and a supported d4 centre; choose c4 or e4 expansion according to Black's setup.", "Challenge the system's centre and coordinate piece development rather than allowing a free fixed setup.", "The bishop develops before e3; central pawn breaks still depend on the opponent's actual moves.", "A system is not a compulsory move order. Watch queenside pawn pressure and bishop exchanges."),
        "Colle System" to g("Build a supported d4/e3 centre, develop the kingside and prepare e4 with enough pieces supporting it.", "Challenge d4 early and develop actively before White completes an unopposed e4 break.", "White's c1 bishop can be restricted until the centre changes; the e4 break is a means of opening it.", "Completing a triangle is not enough if the opponent has already increased pressure on d4."),
        "Rapport-Jobava" to g("Develop Nc3/Bf4 actively and examine e4 or Nb5 ideas only when supported by the actual position.", "Challenge the centre and cover concrete knight incursions while developing normally.", "Early Nc3 blocks the c-pawn and makes e4-based expansion more significant than in some London setups.", "Do not play a memorized Nb5 attack if c7 is adequately defended or the centre needs attention."),
        "King's Indian Attack" to g("Coordinate a kingside fianchetto, d3/e4 centre and development; choose an e5 or queenside plan according to Black's structure.", "Use central space and counterplay before White's pieces are ready for a kingside expansion.", "Similar piece setups can meet very different black pawn chains; identify the current centre first.", "A standard setup does not justify an automatic kingside pawn storm."),
        "Bird Opening" to g("Use f4 to influence e5, develop safely and choose a central structure that supports the advanced f-pawn.", "Challenge the centre and inspect the kingside weaknesses created by the f-pawn advance.", "The f-pawn advance changes the e1–h4 diagonal and the king's pawn shelter.", "Early checks and gambit replies require calculation before routine development."),
        "Nimzo-Larsen" to g("Develop Bb2 toward the long diagonal and contest the centre with coordinated pieces and timely pawn breaks.", "Occupy the centre while limiting the b2 bishop's influence; avoid leaving central pawns unsupported.", "The bishop's effectiveness depends on the central pawns and long-diagonal exchanges.", "A fianchetto is not active simply because it exists; check blockers on the diagonal."),
        "Zukertort" to g("Use flexible knight development to choose a queen-pawn setup or a fianchetto according to Black's centre.", "Develop and contest central squares while remaining ready for d4 or c4 transpositions.", "The initial knight move does not fix the final pawn structure.", "Do not select a middlegame plan from the starting name alone; inspect the actual centre."),
        "Bishop's Opening" to g("Use Bc4 pressure with sound development and a supported central break.", "Develop against the active bishop and seek a well-supported ...d5 challenge.", "Quiet d3 setups and open d4 setups require different timing.", "Pressure on f7 alone is not a forcing attack."),
        "Ponziani" to g("Use c3 to prepare d4, while calculating Black's immediate counterplay against e4.", "Challenge the centre promptly and develop actively against White's preparatory c-pawn move.", "The c3–d4 plan can gain space but costs a developing tempo.", "An early central challenge can demand concrete defence rather than routine d4."),
        "Center Game" to g("Use the early d4 exchange to open central lines and gain activity without unnecessary queen moves.", "Develop with tempo against any early queen recapture and contest the opened centre.", "Early central pawn exchanges expose files and reward development.", "A central queen can become a target; avoid confusing central placement with secure activity."),
        "Danish Gambit" to g("Use the pawn investment for rapid bishop development and open lines, calculating whether the attack compensates for material.", "Decide whether to retain or return material to finish development and reduce the attack.", "Multiple central pawn exchanges open diagonals quickly.", "Open bishops alone do not prove compensation; check forcing replies and queen trades."),
        "Blackmar-Diemer" to g("Use rapid development and open lines to justify any pawn investment, checking king safety and concrete tactics.", "Complete development and neutralize the initiative; returning a pawn can be preferable to keeping it at a large cost in tempi.", "Central pawn exchanges can leave White active pieces but a material deficit.", "Do not label an attacking setup a forced win without calculating the defence."),
        "Indian Defense" to g("Build a supported centre and identify which Indian setup Black actually chooses before committing to a wing plan.", "Use flexible knight development and choose a concrete central challenge consistent with your bishop setup.", "Many Indian positions transpose; the central pawns and bishop diagonals determine the plan.", "The generic name is not a complete repertoire; follow the named continuations and actual board."),
        "Queen's Pawn" to g("Support d4 and develop efficiently; decide whether c4 or e4 is the useful central challenge.", "Contest White's central space with supported pawn breaks and active development.", "Queen-pawn setups can transpose into gambits, systems or Indian defences.", "Avoid fixed moves when the opponent's pressure changes the requirements of the position."),
        "King's Pawn" to g("Use e4 to support development and central control; choose d4 timing according to Black's reply.", "Challenge e4 while developing and choosing a sustainable central structure.", "The opponent's reply determines whether the centre is open, closed or asymmetric.", "Do not confuse the broad starting family with a fully specified opening line."),
    )
    fun forFamily(name: String): OpeningGuide = guides.entries.firstOrNull { name.startsWith(it.key) }?.value ?: OpeningGuide(
        "Develop toward central control, compare the supported d4/e4 breaks and keep the king safe. Use the move-by-move board facts to adapt to this unusual setup.",
        "Contest the centre with supported pawn breaks and coordinated development. Inspect the actual threats before adopting a familiar setup.",
        "This family currently uses general positional guidance. Central pawns, open files and piece activity—not the name alone—determine the plan.",
        "Unusual move orders may leave pieces or king squares vulnerable. Use bounded engine analysis for concrete alternatives; no universal winning plan is claimed.", false)

    /** Branch-specific focus supplements, rather than replacing, the underlying family plans. */
    fun routeFocus(family: String, name: String): String {
        val text = name.lowercase()
        return when {
            family == "Ruy Lopez" && "berlin" in text -> "Berlin focus: Black counterattacks e4 with the knight. If queens are exchanged, compare White's kingside pawn majority with Black's bishop activity and king coordination; not every Berlin route reaches that endgame."
            family == "Ruy Lopez" && "marshall" in text -> "Marshall focus: the ...d5 pawn investment aims for piece activity. Compare White's defensive development with Black's attacking tempi; the sacrifice is not a guaranteed win. Anti-Marshall move orders may avoid this structure."
            family == "Ruy Lopez" && "exchange" in text -> "Exchange focus: White can trade the Spanish bishop for a changed black c-pawn structure. Compare pawn-majority prospects with the bishop pair and open lines, not only the doubled pawns."
            family == "Ruy Lopez" && "breyer" in text -> "Breyer focus: Black's knight can regroup via b8 and d7, allowing other pieces to develop. White uses the centre while checking pressure on e4."
            family == "Ruy Lopez" && "chigorin" in text -> "Chigorin focus: ...Na5 can chase the Spanish bishop and support queenside expansion. Compare the resulting d4 pressure with White's space and bishop activity."
            family == "Ruy Lopez" && "zaitsev" in text -> "Zaitsev focus: the b7 bishop and e-file rook can increase central pressure. White must support e4 while developing and retaining useful tension."
            family == "Ruy Lopez" && "open" in text -> "Open Spanish focus: early ...Nxe4 and central exchanges make piece activity and the d5 pawn important. Do not use a slow closed-Spanish plan without reassessing."
            family == "Ruy Lopez" && ("schliemann" in text || "jaenisch" in text) -> "Early ...f5 focus: the f-pawn challenge can open forcing central play but changes Black's king shelter. Follow checks and exchanges concretely."
            family == "Sicilian Defense" && "dragon" in text -> "Dragon focus: the g7 bishop's long diagonal and c-file pressure compete with White's kingside play. Accelerated move orders can change the timing of ...d5."
            family == "Sicilian Defense" && "najdorf" in text -> "Najdorf focus: ...a6 keeps Black's central choices flexible and limits some b5 incursions. Compare ...e5 structures with ...e6 setups; White's chosen bishop and pawn setup determine the attack."
            family == "Sicilian Defense" && ("sveshnikov" in text || "pelikan" in text) -> "Sveshnikov focus: the ...e5 advance creates space and a potential d5 outpost. White seeks control of that square while Black uses activity and queenside space."
            family == "Sicilian Defense" && "alapin" in text -> "Alapin focus: c3 prepares a pawn recapture on d4 instead of an open-Sicilian knight recapture. Check the immediate pressure on e4 before building the centre."
            family == "Sicilian Defense" && "rossolimo" in text -> "Rossolimo focus: Bb5 lets White compare the bishop pair with changes to Black's pawn structure. The recapture determines the resulting files and weaknesses."
            family == "Sicilian Defense" && "closed" in text -> "Closed Sicilian focus: White delays d4 and may use a kingside fianchetto. Prepare a useful central or wing break; the open-Sicilian development plan does not automatically apply."
            family == "Sicilian Defense" && ("taimanov" in text || "kan" in text) -> "Flexible ...e6 focus: Black postpones some commitments while developing and preparing ...d5. White should use development to keep the centre under control."
            family == "French Defense" && "winawer" in text -> "Winawer focus: ...Bb4 pressures the c3 knight. If Black exchanges there, compare White's bishop pair and kingside chances with the changed c-pawns and central targets."
            family == "French Defense" && "advance" in text -> "Advance focus: White's d4/e5 chain gains space; Black can attack d4 with ...c5 and e5 with a prepared ...f6. The pawn-chain base and head are different targets."
            family == "French Defense" && "tarrasch" in text -> "Tarrasch focus: Nd2 avoids some c3 pinning ideas but temporarily blocks the c1 bishop. Central exchanges determine whether an isolated pawn or a closed chain remains."
            family == "French Defense" && "exchange" in text -> "Exchange focus: exchanging e/d pawns removes the usual locked French chain. Compare open-file activity and development instead of assuming an Advance-style space advantage."
            family == "Caro-Kann Defense" && "panov" in text -> "Panov focus: c4 challenges the centre and can lead to an isolated d-pawn. White seeks activity while Black compares blockade and piece exchanges."
            family == "Caro-Kann Defense" && "advance" in text -> "Advance focus: the bishop often develops before ...e6. White gains space, while Black challenges the base of the centre; the bishop can also become a target."
            family == "Caro-Kann Defense" && "exchange" in text -> "Exchange focus: cxd5 leaves Black's c-file open and a d5 pawn. This differs from the symmetrical French Exchange pawn structure."
            family == "Queen's Gambit Declined" && "exchange" in text -> "Exchange focus: a Carlsbad structure can support a minority attack or a prepared central expansion for White. Black needs active counterplay rather than only pawn defence."
            family == "King's Indian Defense" && "fianchetto" in text -> "Fianchetto focus: White's g2 bishop changes kingside defensive resources. Reassess central and queenside breaks instead of automatically starting a classical kingside pawn storm."
            family == "King's Indian Defense" && ("sämisch" in text || "saemisch" in text) -> "Sämisch focus: f3 supports the centre but delays the g1 knight. Compare central stability and king placement before a wing attack."
            family == "Nimzo-Indian Defense" && "classical" in text -> "Classical Qc2 focus: the queen may recapture on c3 and preserve the pawn structure, at a cost in development tempi. Black can use those tempi to challenge the centre."
            family == "Nimzo-Indian Defense" && "rubinstein" in text -> "Rubinstein focus: e3 supports development and d4. If Black exchanges on c3, compare White's bishop pair with the resulting c-pawn structure."
            family.startsWith("Dutch") && "stonewall" in text -> "Stonewall focus: the fixed dark-square chain supports space but leaves light-square holes and can restrict the c8 bishop. Bishop exchanges and central breaks determine the plan."
            "gambit" in text -> "Gambit focus: identify the actual material investment and what development or open lines it buys. Keeping or returning material is a concrete decision; the name does not establish compensation."
            "exchange" in text -> "Exchange focus: compare the structure and piece activity before and after the named exchange. A simplified position still requires an active plan."
            "fianchetto" in text -> "Fianchetto focus: inspect blockers on the long diagonal and choose central exchanges that give the bishop a useful role."
            else -> "Line focus: follow the exact pawn structure and piece roles shown by the board observations. The named route is one continuation in this family, not a forced sequence against every reply."
        }
    }
}
